    "summary": "The flag is green as of 3:11 PM ET. Calling it off because: high thunderstorm potential. Rain chance peaks around 51% near 3 PM; get out of the water at the first thunder. County rip current risk is rated High (information only). No water quality advisories nearby.",
    "explanation": "Not today. High thunderstorm potential. This is the kind of thing that genuinely changes during the day, so it's worth a re-check before you write the afternoon off entirely.\n\nStorms are the thing to watch. Rain chances climb through the afternoon, running highest from about 3 PM to 6 PM and peaking near 51% around 3 PM. That is the normal South Florida summer pattern rather than a washout - it usually means a cell builds, rains hard somewhere nearby for twenty minutes, and moves on. The rule that matters is lightning: flags say nothing about it, so get out of the water at the first rumble and wait half an hour after the last one.\n\nFor what it's worth, the county rip current risk is rated high. That number is a forecast written before dawn for the whole coastline and it regularly disagrees with the flag actually flying at Pompano, so treat it as background, not a veto."
